Cell["\<\
Forces and fluxes can drive an inspiral together, for example a \
gravitational-wave flux and gas drag.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Special orbits", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
The radial equations are regular for circular orbits, and the equation for x \
is regular at x = 0, so an inspiral can pass from prograde to retrograde. \
Orbits that start exactly polar, x = 0, are not supported. Schwarzschild is a \
= 0.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Equatorial orbits, x = \[PlusMinus]1, are a special case: x stays \
\[PlusMinus]1 and \[Theta] = \[Pi]/2, so the force must have no \[Theta] \
component in the plane, and a flux must not change x. An orbit that starts \
circular stays circular under a flux. The Darwin phase is undefined on a \
circular orbit, and the Hughes phase on an equatorial one:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"circular", "=", 
   RowBox[{"OsculatingInspiral", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Association", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"\"\<a\>\"", "\[Rule]", "0"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<\[Epsilon]\>\"", "\[Rule]", 
        FractionBox["1", 
         SuperscriptBox["10", "2"]]}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<p\>\"", "\[Rule]", "12"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<e\>\"", "\[Rule]", "0"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<x\>\"", "\[Rule]", "1"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<Flux\>\"", "\[Rule]", "\"\<Peters\>\""}]}], "]"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"IntegrationLimit", "\[Rule]", "1000"}]}], "]"}]}], 
  ";"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"circular", "[", "\"\<e\>\"", "]"}], "[", "1000", "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"circular", "[", "\"\<x\>\"", "]"}], "[", "1000", "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"circular", "[", "\"\<\[Psi]r\>\"", "]"}], "[", "1000", "]"}], 
   ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"circular", "[", "\"\<\[Psi]\[Theta]\>\"", "]"}], "[", "1000", 
    "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"0.`", ",", "1.`", ",", 
   RowBox[{"Missing", "[", "\<\"UndefinedAtZeroEccentricity\"\>", "]"}], ",", 
   
   RowBox[{"Missing", "[", "\<\"UndefinedForEquatorialOrbit\"\>", "]"}]}], 
  "}"}]], "Output"],

Cell["Frequencies and angles", "Subsection"],

Cell["\<\
The inspiral also gives the Boyer-Lindquist frequencies of the osculating \
geodesic, \[CapitalOmega]r, \[CapitalOmega]\[Theta] and \
\[CapitalOmega]\[Phi], from KerrGeodesics, and its Boyer-Lindquist action \
angles \[CapitalPhi]r, \[CapitalPhi]\[Theta] and \[CapitalPhi]\[Phi] (Lynch \
and Burke 2025). Along a geodesic each angle advances at its frequency. Over \
an inspiral the angles accumulate, and differ from the integral of the \
frequencies by the force's effect on them, which is small until the orbit \
nears the separatrix. Here at t = 2000, before the plunge:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Plot", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Evaluate", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"inspiral", "[", "#1", "]"}], "[", "t", "]"}], "&"}], ")"}], "/@", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
      "\"\<\[CapitalOmega]r\>\"", ",", "\"\<\[CapitalOmega]\[Theta]\>\"", 
       ",", "\"\<\[CapitalOmega]\[Phi]\>\""}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"t", ",", "0", ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"inspiral", "[", "\"\<Domain\>\"", "]"}], 
      "\[LeftDoubleBracket]", "2", "\[RightDoubleBracket]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"PlotLegends", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
     "\"\<\[CapitalOmega]r\>\"", ",", "\"\<\[CapitalOmega]\[Theta]\>\"", ",", 
      "\"\<\[CapitalOmega]\[Phi]\>\""}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"AxesLabel", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<t\>\"", ",", "None"}], "}"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

